{{~##
   What every generated Java file starts with: the banner, the package and the imports.

   The imports are per file, from TypeDependencies plus whatever the file's own text reaches
   for. javac does not warn about an unused one, so this could hand every file the same list -
   but a list that says what the file needs is worth having, and it is no more work.

   Nothing imports another generated type: they are all one package, so a table's file names
   another table's record type with no import at all. Same as Go, and for the same reason.

   A blank entry in `imports` is the gap between the java.* group and the rest, which is how
   Java code is conventionally laid out.
##~}}
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Generated by Tabbit. DO NOT EDIT.
//
// Changes to this file may cause incorrect behavior and will be lost if the code is
// regenerated.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

package {{ package_name }};
{{~ if imports | array.size > 0 ~}}

{{~ for line in imports ~}}
{{ line }}
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ end ~}}

